Compute the community dissimilarity (default Bray-Curtis) between every
sample and a \strong{reference} (e.g. a control), then visualize and test which
kinds of samples sit closest to that reference. The reference is either a
single sample given by name (\code{ref_sample}) or a set of samples flagged in
\code{sample_data} (\code{ref_fact} + \code{ref_value}); with several references, the
per-sample distance to the set is aggregated with \code{ref_agg}.

Four complementary views are returned:
\enumerate{
\item a violin + jitter plot of the distance to the reference per level of
\code{fact} (levels ordered from closest to farthest), with a Kruskal-Wallis
test reported in the subtitle;
\item a ranked lollipop of all samples (closest to the reference first);
\item a \strong{global} ordination (context): all samples, with the \code{n_nearest}
closest ones highlighted and joined to the reference by spokes;
\item a \strong{local} ordination recomputed on the \code{n_nearest} closest samples
only, revealing the structure \emph{among} the close samples (a global
ordination is dominated by distances between far samples and is often
uninformative for the reference question).
}
}
\details{
No normalisation is done inside the function: rarefy or transform \code{physeq}
